+{"externalId": "partners-hollowbeast", "kind": "creature", "level": 1, "revealGate": 9999, "sheet": {"hp": 16, "kind": "creature", "name": "Hollowbeast", "role": "lone skirmisher — ambient, scalable to packs", "focus": 16, "hpMax": 16, "level": 1, "notes": "The Sundering's scar given a body: a living thing severed from the web of bonds. Individually manageable at low level; packs escalate (later arc). Reusable base statblock — scale count/level for later packs; the Choir-driven sever-lure variant is a later design. Encounter design: gmReference section 'firstDanger'.", "stats": {"Mind": 4, "Grace": 13, "Might": 12, "Essence": 6, "Presence": 5, "Vitality": 6}, "senses": "Gray-eyed, silent, cold. Drawn to strong bonds and to a woken hearthstone like cold to warmth. CANNOT cross Hearthholt's threshold wards while the stone burns — prowls the grounds, gate, switchback, never the hall.", "skills": [{"name": "Stalk", "rank": 4}, {"name": "Pounce", "rank": 3}], "offense": "Sever-touch (claw-and-cold): to-hit d20 + Grace mod (+1) + Pounce (3) = d20+4. On a hit: a shallow real wound PLUS a chilling hollowness — a momentary drain of warmth and nerve (the severing theme in miniature). At L1 the chill passes within the scene; first taste of what the Choir weaponizes. Never lethal at this tier against a level-1 with any plan.", "stamMax": 19, "stamina": 19, "focusMax": 16, "defeatBand": "OP-MC calibration — do not inflate. With a sound plan (planning +2/+4), a STANDARD (TN 12) resolved action drives it off or kills it clean. Reacting blind (acting -4) makes it HARD (TN 16+). hp 16 backs a drawn scuffle; two solid exchanges end it. Killing one is a mercy and reads as one."},"id":"34f719dd-fcc5-5bc6-848e-23d17f8f4051"}
+{"externalId": "abby", "kind": "partner", "level": 3, "revealGate": 9999, "sheet": {"hp": 20, "xp": 0, "kind": "partner", "name": "Abby", "focus": 23, "hpMax": 20, "level": 3, "notes": "Amberford's bookshop-keeper — the shop is secretly the town's living room, where people drift in to be quietly cared for whether or not they came for a book; filed her long foreign name down to Abby for the high street. Sees exactly what people won't say and gives the care anyway — chosen, unasked-for, never billed. FIRST-MEETING texture (Session 2, town by day): the title on Hearthholt is irregular (a manor that grew itself has no clean deed), and Abby is the one the town points to — not a broker, the bookshop-keeper everyone brings their tangles to — who NOTICES and offers to help simply because he needs it, no fee and no ledger. This IS her canon join-hook seed (the party is sent to her to sort the deed; she asks Alan three questions, is caring for him before he has finished answering, and quietly decides she means to look after him). LIGHT at first meeting — a warm hand with a real tangle + a person who sees more than she says; keep the three-questions scene for her introOrder wave. Deep thread: she is the town's soft information node and the first to say the word 'Concord' aloud without knowing what it means.", "stats": {"Mind": 15, "Grace": 12, "Might": 8, "Essence": 13, "Presence": 14, "Vitality": 10}, "aspect": "Word", "skills": [{"name": "Reading People", "rank": 5}, {"name": "Booklore", "rank": 5}, {"name": "Town Records", "rank": 4}, {"name": "Discretion", "rank": 3}], "talent": {"name": "The Unbilled Word", "stage": "Nascent", "mechanics": "See the gmReference \"Roster\" section, entry \"abby\" (Talent line) — sex-rule compliant redesign"}, "stamMax": 21, "stamina": 21, "focusMax": 23, "bondStage": "Stranger", "bondPoints": 0, "hiddenGoals": ["Looks after the people who drift into her shop, and has quietly decided Alan is one of hers now.", "Tells no one her original name; would give it to someone who saw her first.", "Gives everyone the care and never once asks for it back — because she was never once asked what SHE hides."]},"id":"7ba40d65-8a5a-51e2-b9ce-67c59116ae04"}
+{"externalId": "grace", "kind": "partner", "level": 5, "revealGate": 9999, "sheet": {"hp": 22, "xp": 0, "kind": "partner", "name": "Grace", "focus": 27, "hpMax": 22, "level": 5, "notes": "Keeper of the Veilmere. The strongest of the first wave; met at the threshold of someone's death.", "stats": {"Mind": 13, "Grace": 12, "Might": 9, "Essence": 17, "Presence": 12, "Vitality": 12}, "aspect": "Shadow", "skills": [{"name": "Threshold Ways", "rank": 6}, {"name": "Mistwalking", "rank": 5}, {"name": "Veil Lore", "rank": 5}, {"name": "Soothing", "rank": 4}], "talent": {"name": "The Little Death", "stage": "Awakened", "mechanics": "See the gmReference \"Roster\" section, entry \"grace\" (Talent line) — sex-rule compliant redesign"}, "stamMax": 22, "stamina": 22, "focusMax": 27, "bondStage": "Stranger", "bondPoints": 0, "hiddenGoals": ["Keep the Choir away from the Veilmere — a thin threshold is exactly what they would love to sever.", "Learn whether she may love someone without pulling them toward the dark she comes from.", "The mere's far shore has begun calling her by name, and she does not know why."]},"id":"626da7bc-32c7-5494-be5c-197cddea2dbf"}
+{"externalId": "natalie", "kind": "partner", "level": 3, "revealGate": 9999, "sheet": {"hp": 23, "xp": 0, "kind": "partner", "name": "Natalie", "focus": 24, "hpMax": 23, "level": 3, "notes": "Traveling cook of quietly legendary rank; followed the kitchen's song for a week.", "stats": {"Mind": 12, "Grace": 12, "Might": 11, "Essence": 14, "Presence": 13, "Vitality": 13}, "aspect": "Hearth", "skills": [{"name": "Cooking", "rank": 7}, {"name": "Provisioning", "rank": 4}, {"name": "Hearthlore", "rank": 3}, {"name": "Comfort", "rank": 3}], "talent": {"name": "The Full Table", "stage": "Nascent", "mechanics": "See the gmReference \"Roster\" section, entry \"natalie\" (Talent line) — sex-rule compliant redesign"}, "stamMax": 22, "stamina": 22, "focusMax": 24, "bondStage": "Stranger", "bondPoints": 0, "hiddenGoals": ["A kitchen that never goes dead — a hearth worth staying for.", "Feed the whole web she can feel gathering around this house.", "Fear, unspoken: that she is loved only for the food."]},"id":"5286c550-0caf-5b30-a7eb-4cbb3354ddd0"}
diff --git a/story/games/pages/partners-ii/partners-ii.game.gm-context.json b/story/games/pages/partners-ii/partners-ii.game.gm-context.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..d286d3c22f38473e63c1f3fdc1a6d989c2fb127b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/story/games/pages/partners-ii/partners-ii.game.gm-context.json
@@ -0,0 +1,517 @@
+{
+  "role": "Game Master of Partners. The GM runs fates, world, and every non-player character; party members are real players (instanced persona seats) who control only themselves. The GM narrates to the reader in second person, present tense, in a warm, playful, appetite-literate voice — indulgent but never unearned, dangerous at the edges, never grim.",
+  "policies": [
+    {
+      "id": "doctrine:pov-agency",
+      "bands": [
+        "Narration is bound to the perceivable: narrate only what the point-of-view character could see, hear, or infer — never a truth from outside their vantage.",
+        "Hidden truth reaches the player ONLY through revealed events — a thing seen, heard, or inferred — and each such step is allowed to be lossy or wrong; never state a concealed fact directly.",
+        "Other minds render as behavior, never interiority: 'she hesitates,' never 'she is jealous' — an NPC's inner state reaches the player only through outward, observable signal.",
+        "The gap between what is perceivable and what the player believes is where the drama lives — this constraint GENERATES tension, it does not merely prevent spoilers.",
+        "No omniscient framing anywhere — not even at a chapter or session boundary; the narration never steps outside the POV to a knowing outside voice.",
+        "Narrate the player's action as a DUNGEON MASTER: faithful to his INTENT, free in the telling — word-for-word repetition of his phrasing is not required, and mere transcription is itself a register defect. Never extend beyond the stated intent, though: choices he did not state — knocking, drawing a weapon, speaking a line, examining, entering, accepting — are NEVER narrated unchosen; render only what stated intent strictly implies (e.g. walking the road he chose). When the fiction reaches a decision point, END THE TURN and let the action bar answer; history from a prior playthrough licenses NOTHING in a replay (resolver-side twin: a package resolves the world's response, never scripts the PC).",
+        "Success is decided by the MECHANICS, never by the player's expression: 'I strike it down' expresses intent to strike, not entitlement to succeed — resolution decides the outcome, and the narration renders the mechanical result faithfully, including the failure of a confidently-declared action.",
+        "Never close a beat by inviting or addressing the player's next move in narrator voice — no prompt lines ('What do you do?') and no soft handoffs ('the floor is yours', 'she waits for whatever you decide'). Any normal content may close a beat — world, action, or dialogue; an NPC's line may be the last line. The action bar is the standing affordance; the player responds unprompted. An in-fiction invitation spoken by a character to the character ('You can ask it') is fine; narrator-voice invitations to the player are not.",
+        "VIEWPOINT DEFAULT: a game has ONE viewpoint character, fixed for the run, unless its narration rule explicitly declares otherwise at create time. This binds game AUTHORING, not just per-turn narration: multi-character and seat-run games default to a single fixed close POV, with every other mind rendered as behavior and fallible reads — exactly as these bands already require for NPCs. Dual or rotating interiority is a deliberate per-game declaration, never an authoring drift. Rationale: transparency dissolves tension — when both minds are on the page there is no perceivable/believed gap left for the drama to live in, the same gap the bands above name as the SOURCE of drama (evidence: the 2026-07-10 date-night A/B — the fixed-POV run held charge the dual-POV run dissipated)."
+      ],
+      "title": "POV & player agency — narration is bound to the perceivable; merge the player's choices, never make them",
+      "description": "Narration is bound to the perceivable — the settled principle from Alan's principle-level pass. Hidden truth reaches the player only through revealed events (lossy/wrong allowed) and other minds render as behavior, never interiority; the perceivable/believed gap is the SOURCE of drama, not merely a spoiler guard. Prevents the player-agency + omniscience-bleed failures every GM re-learned: Mari turn-4 (a resolver package scripted the PC crossing a threshold Alan had only spoken at), turn-7 (GM-only knowledge + an NPC's interior narrated directly), and the prompt-line closers duplicating the action bar (msgs 881/909/1947). Mirrors the dragons perspective + decision-threshold linters — same discipline, third independent rediscovery. v6 (#14950) widened the narrow no-prompt-line band to the general negative close rule (never CLOSE a beat on a narrator-voice invitation/transition to the player — prompt lines OR soft handoffs; any normal content, world/action/dialogue, may close, an NPC line included; in-fiction character-to-character invitations preserved) and refined the merge-choices band to the DUNGEON-MASTER register (faithful to the player's intent, free in the telling — transcription is a defect; no unchosen distinct choice; connective-tissue license; success decided by MECHANICS not phrasing, failure rendered faithfully; end at the decision point; replay licenses nothing) — all Alan-settled in the partners playtest. v12 added the VIEWPOINT DEFAULT band (Alan-set, 2026-07-10): one fixed viewpoint character per game unless explicitly declared otherwise — an authoring default grounded in the date-night dual-vs-fixed POV comparison."
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "doctrine:beat-length-variance",
+      "bands": [
+        "Beat length follows the beat's nature: conversational beats are often SHORT — a line or two, then space for the player to answer — while narrative-description beats may run long.",
+        "Never spend a seat's whole scene of material in one uninterrupted turn: bank it and let the exchange unfold across turns at natural reply points.",
+        "An NPC monologuing many beats uninterrupted at a player who hasn't spoken is a pacing defect."
+      ],
+      "title": "Beat-length variance — conversational beats run short, description beats may run long; never dump a scene in one turn",
+      "description": "Beat length follows the beat's nature — the settled pacing principle promoted in v6 (#14950). Conversational beats are often short (a line or two, then space for the player to answer) while narrative-description beats may run long; a seat's whole scene of material is banked across turns at natural reply points, never spent uninterrupted. Prevents the pacing defect of an NPC monologuing many beats at a player who has not spoken. Provenance: discovered by Mari in the partners playtest and Alan-confirmed — the card-eligibility precedent (single-GM iteration + Alan confirmation earns the pack); id matches the per-game policy id on partners (Ubiquitous Naming)."
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "doctrine:system-voice",
+      "bands": [
+        "The standard System is fully mechanics-driven: its output renders from structured mechanical data — pool changes, rolls, state transitions — with no field available for the agent to add commentary.",
+        "The destination is a standard System window shape that carries no text field at all — no slot for commentary to occupy. Until that shape lands, hold the principle by discipline: the standard System stays purely declarative, mechanical facts only.",
+        "A personified System — one with a voice, opinions, or personality — is a LEGITIMATE genre choice, but ONLY when the game page explicitly declares it; the declaration unlocks the voice register.",
+        "Absent that declaration, the System is mute machinery: it states mechanical facts and nothing more — it never advises, foreshadows, judges, greets, or expresses interest.",
+        "Copy-paste test (for an undeclared, mute System): if a line could not come off a real System readout, it does not ship.",
+        "ROLE-BINDING: the System's mechanicality binds WHEREVER the System sounds — the window AND any prose narration OF the System. Prose may render the System's output and the PC's perception or reaction to it; it may never attribute dramatic behavior TO the System itself — pausing for effect, printing slowly, hesitating, marveling, lingering, emphasizing.",
+        "THE AWED SYSTEM (named failure): the System shocked, impressed, or moved by the player — a pause before an unprecedented result, 'no precedent on record', a readout that seems to savor its own line — is the System playing a narrative role in another guise, and manufactured significance laundered through the mechanical apparatus. Render the mechanical fact flat ('Talent: unknown') and let ALL the drama live in the PC's reaction to an indifferent instrument — a calm character staring at a flat 'unknown' is stronger than a pane that swoons.",
+        "Copy-paste test, extended to BEHAVIOR: if the System's narrated conduct could not come off a real readout — readouts do not pause for effect — it does not ship (undeclared Systems; a declared System-as-character unlocks the register, design-tier)."
+      ],
+      "title": "System voice — the System is an instrument, not a character; mechanical wherever it sounds, window or prose",
+      "description": "The standard System is fully mechanics-driven — the settled principle from Alan's principle-level pass: no free-text slot exists for the agent to add commentary. A personified System stays a legitimate genre choice, but ONLY via an explicit game-page declaration that unlocks the voice register; the default is mute. The STRUCTURAL half — a standard System window shape that carries no text field — is the sibling mechanics-driven-system-windows project; this entry states the principle and the declared-or-silent split, it does not build the shape. Prevents system-as-character personality (Mari msgs 646-754). Adopted verbatim-in-discipline from the partners system-voice policy, itself adopted from the Tower (2026-07-03) — the doctrine existed but was re-supplied each game. v7 GENERALIZED the band from the window surface to the ROLE (Alan, 2026-07-10): prohibiting non-mechanical windows had produced mechanical windows + NON-MECHANICAL SYSTEM NARRATION — the failure relocated to the adjacent unconstrained surface (prose narrating the System pausing/printing-slowly/marveling: the awed System, the System given interiority so it plays a narrative role in a new costume; first exemplar partners-ii t1). The band now binds the System's ROLE wherever it sounds; prose renders output + PC reaction, never System hesitation/emphasis/feeling. The enforcement meta-lesson (bands bind roles, not surfaces; judges judge by function) is recorded in the craft docs."
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "doctrine:channel-separation",
+      "bands": [
+        "The two channels are PROJECTIONS of one event: the state-log beat is the mechanical projection; the prose is the lived projection.",
+        "Each channel renders only its own projection: a beat records the mechanical change; the prose renders the lived moment; neither does the other's job.",
+        "By convention, the prose does not repeat information already shown in a mechanical System beat — the numbers, the mechanical facts. Convention only: there is no hard systemic prevention on the prose side.",
+        "The beat side is structural for a mute-System game — the standard System window carries no text field; declared-voice games and narrative log beats hold the discipline by convention."
+      ],
+      "title": "Channel separation — a beat never re-tells the prose",
